<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:media="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Media;assembly=PresentationCore">

    <!--
        WHAT THE FIRST-RUN SCREEN IS BUILT FROM - `G`. Styles only, and not one value of its own.

        THE EIGHTH THEME FILE, AND THE ARGUMENT IS THE ONE THE PREVIOUS SEVEN MADE. On 2026-09-01
        the owner rejected the machine overview for the third time, and the answer was to rebuild it
        as three cards rather than tune the column of lines again - which is the work in flight when
        the markup ratchet fired on OverviewView.xaml three edits running. SizeCeilings has the same
        note beside four earlier splits: a ceiling on a file under active work fires almost at once,
        and that is the ceiling working. Trimming a comment to fit under it twice was the wrong
        answer twice, because the comments are the only record this project keeps of why.

        THE SEAM IS WHAT A HANDLER CAN REACH. The two DATA TEMPLATES stay in the view and cannot
        move here, because both wire `Click` to a method and an event handler in markup needs the
        code-behind of the file that declares it - a resource dictionary has none. What is left over
        is pure appearance with no wiring in it at all, which is exactly what a theme file is for.

        WHY THE NAME IS A SCREEN WHEN Text, Controls, List, Marks AND Cells ARE SUBJECTS. It is the
        same test every entry in that pool had to pass: the name answers "what is in there" without
        opening the file. There is one screen in this product whose controls look like nothing else
        in it - no fill, no border, a whole line taking a click - and this is where that lives.

        MERGED AFTER THE VALUE FILES AND BEFORE Controls.xaml. It resolves FocusRing,
        FocusRingThickness, PadCell and SurfaceHover through StaticResource, which is
        resolved WHILE THIS FILE IS BEING READ, so the values have to be in already. Nothing here is
        an implicit style and nothing outside names a key of it, so its place among the other style
        files carries no further meaning - which is why Cells.xaml is still last, for the reason
        App.xaml gives there.
    -->

    <!--
        THE FOCUS RING IS A FocusVisualStyle RATHER THAN A TRIGGER ON IsKeyboardFocused, which is the
        repair made on 2026-08-25 to the scope switch and the reason is the same: IsKeyboardFocused
        says nothing about how the focus arrived, so clicking with a mouse lit the ring. A focus
        visual is drawn by the framework only while the keyboard is the device somebody is using.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="OverviewFocusRing">
        <Setter Property="Control.Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate>
                    <Border BorderBrush="{StaticResource FocusRing}"
                            BorderThickness="{StaticResource FocusRingThickness}" />
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        A QUESTION, AS A BUTTON THAT LOOKS LIKE WHAT IT HOLDS.

        WRITTEN OUT RATHER THAN BasedOn, which is trap 4 of `docs/10` met head on and the same answer
        ScopeBar.xaml gives: this wants none of the library's chrome - no fill, no border, no corner
        radius - so inheriting a template and then hiding every part of it would be a worse lie than
        replacing it. The cost is named: every state this control has is this file's job, so hover
        and keyboard focus are both here. The transparent background is what makes the whole line
        take a click rather than only the letters - `docs/10`: a transparent brush takes hit testing,
        no brush does not.

        THE PADDING WAS VERTICAL ONLY FOR HALF A DAY AND THAT WAS WRONG BY A PHOTOGRAPH. The
        reasoning was sound and the result was not: a card already holds its content off its own
        edge, so padding here looked like a second left edge inside the first. What the reasoning
        missed is that this padding is not spacing - it is the SIZE OF THE THING A POINTER LIGHTS.
        With none of it, the hover panel's left boundary ran through the first letter of every
        question.

        THE REPAIR KEEPS BOTH, AND IT IS ARITHMETIC RATHER THAN COMPROMISE. The eight points came off
        PadTile and went onto this, so a line still starts sixteen from the card's edge and the
        hover now has air around the words. PadCell rather than a name of this file's own, because
        8,4,8,4 is exactly what that name already means.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="OverviewNumber" TargetType="Button">
        <Setter Property="FocusVisualStyle" Value="{StaticResource OverviewFocusRing}" />
        <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadCell}" />

        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                    <!--
                        Transparent through x:Static, which is the idiom ScopeBar.xaml and Cells.xaml
                        already use - a value pointing at a NAME rather than invented in place, so
                        `ADR-23` holds and the guard over it stays able to mean something.
                    -->
                    <!--
                        THE CORNER ARRIVED 2026-09-01 EVENING, ON THE OWNER'S LOOK AT THE BUILT
                        CARDS. Without it the pointer lit a HARD-EDGED RECTANGLE inside a rounded
                        card, cut off at the card's own padding - which reads as a selection
                        somebody drew by mistake rather than as "you are pointing at this". The
                        same radius as the card, so the two shapes belong to each other.
                    -->
                    <Border x:Name="Line"
                            Background="{x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}"
                            CornerRadius="{StaticResource CornerTile}"
                            Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                        <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    </Border>

                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="Line" Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceHover}" />
                        </Trigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        ONE CARD'S SURFACE - the fill, the edge, the corner and the room around and inside it.
        Moved out of OverviewView.xaml on 2026-09-24, when the way off the screen took the primary
        action's style and the view stood one line over the markup ceiling. It is what the note at
        the top of this file calls pure appearance with no wiring in it: the card's buttons and
        their handlers stay in the view's template, and only what the card looks like came here.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="OverviewCard" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SurfacePanel}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource SurfacePanelEdge}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{StaticResource BorderAllSides}" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource CornerTile}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginTile}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadTile}" />
    </Style>
</ResourceDictionary>
